---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import CelticTool from "../tools/CelticTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="celtic">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        A tree whose branches reach up into a ring and whose roots reach down into the same ring, so the whole thing
        is <strong class="text-slate-300">one closed piece</strong>. Four border styles, leaves that are never smaller
        than the beam that makes them, and two feet to stand it in — with every overlap merged away before anything is
        exported, because a line crossing another line is, to a laser, a pair of cuts through both.
    </Fragment>

    <CelticTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </CelticTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Overlaps are joins, not cuts</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    This is the design that most reliably comes off the bed in pieces, and the reason is always the
                    same. A tree of life is branches crossing a trunk, crossing each other, crossing their own leaves
                    and running into a ring. Drawn honestly as outlines, every one of those crossings is a
                    <em>pair of cut lines through both shapes</em> — so the laser dutifully cuts the branch off the
                    trunk on its way past, and the piece arrives as confetti.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So nothing here is drawn as an outline. The generator produces
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">centrelines with widths</strong> — the trunk, every branch, every
                    root — plus leaves, a ring and the tabs, and the tool paints the lot onto a canvas and traces the
                    result exactly <em>once</em>. What comes back is the silhouette of the union: branch meets trunk
                    with no seam, leaf overlaps leaf with no seam, and the only closed curves in the export are the
                    real edge of the real piece and the real holes between the branches. The status bar counts them,
                    so you can see there is one of each rather than one per crossing.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    One thing is deliberately kept out of that union: the border decoration. A braid engraved inside
                    the band is a picture on the material. Painted into the silhouette it would become holes, and a
                    band with holes in it is a band that is not there — so it is a separate engraved layer, and it is
                    the only thing in the drawing that is not red.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">There is a floor under the leaf size</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A tree with a hundred tiny leaves looks wonderful on screen and comes out of a laser as a hundred
                    identical scorch marks, because each leaf is smaller than the kerf plus the char around it. The
                    beam is about a tenth of a millimetre and so is the burn it leaves, and a leaf has to be a good
                    deal bigger than the hole that makes it.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So the floor is <strong class="text-slate-300">enforced rather than suggested</strong>. Ask for
                    2 mm leaves and you get 4 mm leaves and a note saying why. The same reasoning is behind the
                    thinnest-twig figure in the status bar: each level of branching is 72 % of its parent's width, so
                    depth is what makes twigs delicate, and the number tells you before the piece does.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Four borders, and they are not all the same strength</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The difference between a plain band, a braid, a rope and a knot is not only decoration. A plain
                        band is the strongest and the dullest; the other three are the same band with line work burnt
                        into it, which costs nothing in strength but does need room — knotwork in a narrow band engraves
                        as a smudge, because the crossings end up closer together than the beam is wide. The number of
                        crossings follows the circumference rather than being a knob, so a knot at 60 mm and one at
                        300 mm are both the right density.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Something to stand it on, and something to put behind it</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Two tabs hang below the disc and two slotted feet take them. The tabs are painted into the
                        disc's own outline rather than glued on afterwards, and the slot is the sheet plus the kerf,
                        because a slot cut to the nominal thickness is a slot the tab does not go into. The optional
                        backing disc is the same circle with nothing cut out of it and the same tabs, so it stands in
                        the same feet directly behind the tree — cut from a contrasting sheet, it stops the openwork
                        showing you the wall.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Every cut line comes out of a trace, so it is only ever as true as the raster under it — about an
                eighth of a millimetre, which is under the kerf and therefore under anything you could measure on the
                finished piece. On a disc over about 200 mm the resolution comes down rather than the canvas growing
                to twenty megapixels, and the accuracy with it.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
